    "message": {
     "content": "### Executive Summary\n\n**Verdict: Negotiate.** Do not accept as-is, and definitely do not walk away. \n\nYou are in a position of strength:\n1. Google **recruited you** while you were passive.\n2. You are already an established **Meta IC6** (level-to-level parity with Google L6).\n3. The offer has glaring gaps you can leverage: a base salary lower than what you make at Meta ($235k vs. $240k), no sign-on bonus ($0), and equity positioned around the middle of the L6 band.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown & The \"Hidden\" Front-Loading Advantage\n\nOn paper, Google\u2019s annualized total compensation appears to be **$552,000/yr** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k/yr flat equity), which only looks ~$26k (5%) higher than your current Meta comp ($526,000).\n\nHowever, Google standard initial grants vest **front-loaded** on a **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** schedule over four years:\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Google Year 1 | Google Year 2 | Google Year 3 | Google Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Bonus** (Target) | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **Initial RSU Vest** | ~$250,000 | $356,400 *(33%)* | $356,400 *(33%)* | $237,600 *(22%)* | $129,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Refresher Stack** | \u2014 | \u2014 | +~$20k\u2013$30k | +~$50k\u2013$70k | +~$80k\u2013$110k |\n| **Total Realized TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400** | **~$638k + refresh** | **~$520k + refresh** | **~$412k + refresh** |\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n- **Year 1 & 2 Reality:** You will actually make **~$638k/yr** in your first two years at Google\u2014an immediate **+$112k/yr (+21%) bump** over Meta.\n- **Years 3 & 4 Cliff:** Because of the front-loading, your initial equity drops sharply in Years 3 and 4. Google uses annual refreshers (which vest 25%/yr over 4 years) to smooth this out, but as the recruiter noted, refreshes are tied to performance.\n\n---\n\n### What Google Left on the Table\n\n1. **Base Salary ($235k):** This is lower than your current $240k base and sits below the midpoint of their posted range ($183k\u2013$271k). Google rarely expects a candidate to take a cut in base.\n2. **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses to close candidates leaving high-paying FAANG roles. At L6, sign-on bonuses typically range between **$50,000 and $100,000+**.\n3. **Equity ($1.08M):** While solid, L6 PM equity grants at Google can reach **$1.25M to $1.4M+** for strong hires or competitive situations.\n\n---\n\n### Exactly What to Ask For\n\nDo not ask for a single lump-sum increase; ask across the specific buckets:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter-Target | Expected Landing Zone |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$260,000** *(within posted band)* | **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000** |\n| **Equity (4-yr GSU)**| $1,080,000 | **$1,300,000** | **$1,180,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** | **$50,000 \u2013 $75,000** |\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy & Talking Points\n\nSchedule a short call with the recruiter rather than just sending an email. Use the following framework:\n\n#### 1. Anchor on enthusiasm, but establish friction\n> *\"I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope and product direction. However, as you know, I wasn't actively looking to leave Meta. I'm in a great position with strong momentum, so for me to make this move, the compensation package needs to clearly justify walking away from my current trajectory.\"*\n\n#### 2. Point out the base salary deficit\n> *\"On the base salary, the current offer ($235k) is actually a step down from my current base of $240k. Given the posted range goes up to $271k, I'd like to see the base at **$260,000**.\"*\n\n#### 3. Address unvested equity and lack of sign-on\n> *\"At Meta, I have a very predictable equity cadence and $200k vesting over the next 12 months. Since there is currently no sign-on bonus to bridge the transition and offset unvested stock, I\u2019d like to see an **$80,000 sign-on bonus**.\"*\n\n#### 4. Push for equity headroom\n> *\"To ensure long-term alignment and make this an unambiguous decision, I would need the initial equity grant increased to **$1.3M**. If we can get close to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n### Final Advice\nBecause you are happy where you are, you hold all the cards. Tech recruiters at Google are accustomed to this exact conversation for senior lateral hires from Meta. They will almost certainly go back to the compensation committee and return with an improved package.",
